1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,647
A tower that leans.

2
00:00:01,846 --> 00:00:03,466
An apple that falls.

3
00:00:03,666 --> 00:00:06,178
A mould that saved millions of lives.

4
00:00:06,378 --> 00:00:08,918
History books call them happy accidents.

5
00:00:09,118 --> 00:00:10,309
And they're right.

6
00:00:10,509 --> 00:00:11,880
They were accidents.

7
00:00:12,080 --> 00:00:13,349
My accidents.

8
00:00:18,349 --> 00:00:20,031
My name doesn't matter.

9
00:00:20,231 --> 00:00:21,988
What matters is the cap.

10
00:00:22,188 --> 00:00:22,862
Red.

11
00:00:23,062 --> 00:00:24,480
Slightly too big.

12
00:00:24,680 --> 00:00:25,613
Remember it.

13
00:00:25,813 --> 00:00:27,676
It's going to be important.

14
00:00:28,476 --> 00:00:32,505
It all started on a rainy Tuesday, at the City Museum of History.

15
00:00:32,705 --> 00:00:34,694
I was there for one reason.

16
00:00:34,894 --> 00:00:36,480
The air conditioning.

17
00:00:36,680 --> 00:00:37,885
Then I saw it.

18
00:00:38,085 --> 00:00:41,057
A strange brass pocket watch, in a glass case.

19
00:00:41,257 --> 00:00:43,246
The sign said: Do not touch.

20
00:00:43,446 --> 00:00:45,082
Origin unknown.

21
00:00:45,682 --> 00:00:46,931
So naturally...

22
00:00:47,131 --> 00:00:48,192
I touched it.

23
00:00:48,692 --> 00:00:51,009
The hands started spinning backwards.

24
00:00:51,209 --> 00:00:52,937
The floor disappeared.

25
00:00:53,137 --> 00:00:55,958
And the whole world folded up like a paper map.

26
00:00:56,558 --> 00:01:00,509
When I opened my eyes, I was standing in a sunny Italian city.

27
00:01:00,709 --> 00:01:01,490
Pisa.

28
00:01:01,690 --> 00:01:03,716
The year eleven seventy eight.

29
00:01:03,916 --> 00:01:07,024
Workers were building a beautiful white bell tower.

30
00:01:07,224 --> 00:01:09,331
Only three floors so far.

31
00:01:09,531 --> 00:01:10,883
Perfectly straight.

32
00:01:11,083 --> 00:01:12,483
Perfectly normal.

33
00:01:13,083 --> 00:01:16,294
A worker shoved a basket of stones into my arms.

34
00:01:16,494 --> 00:01:18,810
I think he thought I was the new guy.

35
00:01:19,010 --> 00:01:20,369
I didn't argue.

36
00:01:20,569 --> 00:01:22,328
Free lunch is free lunch.

37
00:01:22,928 --> 00:01:25,319
Here's something they don't tell you in school.

38
00:01:25,519 --> 00:01:27,612
The ground in Pisa is soft.

39
00:01:27,812 --> 00:01:31,571
Clay, sand and seashells, left behind by ancient rivers.

40
00:01:31,771 --> 00:01:35,837
Building a stone tower there is like putting a fridge on a mattress.

41
00:01:36,437 --> 00:01:37,149
And me?

42
00:01:37,349 --> 00:01:38,974
I was carrying stones.

43
00:01:39,174 --> 00:01:40,544
Lots of stones.

44
00:01:40,744 --> 00:01:42,591
Always to the south side.

45
00:01:42,791 --> 00:01:44,375
Every single trip.

46
00:01:44,575 --> 00:01:45,749
Same side.

47
00:01:45,949 --> 00:01:48,190
I was very proud of my system.

48
00:01:48,690 --> 00:01:50,810
Then came a long, deep groan.

49
00:01:51,010 --> 00:01:52,874
The whole tower tilted.

50
00:01:53,074 --> 00:01:54,140
Just a little.

51
00:01:54,340 --> 00:01:55,263
Just enough.

52
00:01:55,463 --> 00:01:57,479
The workers stared at the tower.

53
00:01:57,679 --> 00:01:59,248
Then they stared at me.

54
00:02:00,048 --> 00:02:03,713
Luckily, a war stopped construction for almost a century.

55
00:02:03,913 --> 00:02:07,212
The soil had time to settle, so the tower never fell.

56
00:02:07,412 --> 00:02:08,511
But the lean?

57
00:02:08,711 --> 00:02:10,384
The lean stayed forever.

58
00:02:10,584 --> 00:02:12,996
Today it leans about four degrees.

59
00:02:13,196 --> 00:02:18,472
Engineers spent eleven years, until two thousand and one, making sure it never falls.

60
00:02:18,672 --> 00:02:20,942
You're welcome, Italian tourism.

61
00:02:21,742 --> 00:02:23,903
Then the watch started ticking again.

62
00:02:24,303 --> 00:02:25,301
Cold air.

63
00:02:25,501 --> 00:02:26,731
Green fields.

64
00:02:26,931 --> 00:02:28,581
A farm in England.

65
00:02:28,781 --> 00:02:31,449
Woolsthorpe Manor, sixteen sixty six.

66
00:02:31,649 --> 00:02:37,256
A terrible plague had closed Cambridge University, so a young student had come home to think.

67
00:02:37,456 --> 00:02:39,529
His name was Isaac Newton.

68
00:02:40,129 --> 00:02:44,258
He was sitting in the orchard, staring at the sky, mumbling about the Moon.

69
00:02:44,458 --> 00:02:48,590
Why does the Moon circle the Earth, instead of flying off into space?

70
00:02:48,790 --> 00:02:49,708
Deep stuff.

71
00:02:49,908 --> 00:02:51,181
I was hungry.

72
00:02:51,781 --> 00:02:55,380
At the very top of the tree hung one perfect red apple.

73
00:02:55,580 --> 00:02:56,612
I climbed.

74
00:02:56,812 --> 00:02:58,250
Branch by branch.

75
00:02:58,450 --> 00:02:59,324
I reached.

76
00:02:59,524 --> 00:03:00,411
I stretched.

77
00:03:00,611 --> 00:03:01,949
And the branch went...

78
00:03:02,149 --> 00:03:02,756
snap.

79
00:03:03,156 --> 00:03:04,597
The apple dropped.

80
00:03:04,797 --> 00:03:05,920
Straight down.

81
00:03:06,120 --> 00:03:07,400
Not sideways.

82
00:03:07,600 --> 00:03:08,422
Not up.

83
00:03:08,622 --> 00:03:09,288
Down.

84
00:03:09,488 --> 00:03:13,011
Newton watched it fall, and something in his eyes changed.

85
00:03:13,611 --> 00:03:15,042
Why always down?

86
00:03:15,242 --> 00:03:20,560
What if the same force that pulls an apple to the ground, reaches all the way up to the Moon?

87
00:03:20,760 --> 00:03:26,657
Twenty one years later, in sixteen eighty seven, he published his law of universal gravitation.

88
00:03:26,857 --> 00:03:28,605
He never even noticed me.

89
00:03:28,805 --> 00:03:30,258
Lying in the grass.

90
00:03:30,458 --> 00:03:32,295
With a branch on my face.

91
00:03:33,095 --> 00:03:33,728
Tick.

92
00:03:33,928 --> 00:03:34,627
Tock.

93
00:03:34,827 --> 00:03:36,175
Here we go again.

94
00:03:36,575 --> 00:03:37,333
Smoke.

95
00:03:37,533 --> 00:03:38,630
Car horns.

96
00:03:38,830 --> 00:03:39,749
Big Ben.

97
00:03:39,949 --> 00:03:41,914
London, nineteen twenty eight.

98
00:03:42,114 --> 00:03:43,881
St Mary's Hospital.

99
00:03:44,081 --> 00:03:47,455
A tiny, messy laboratory, full of glass dishes.

100
00:03:47,655 --> 00:03:52,688
A Scottish scientist named Alexander Fleming was packing for his summer holiday.

101
00:03:53,288 --> 00:03:58,300
In those dishes, he was growing bacteria, the kind that cause nasty infections.

102
00:03:58,500 --> 00:04:00,972
Back then, a small scratch could kill you.

103
00:04:01,172 --> 00:04:02,497
There was no cure.

104
00:04:02,697 --> 00:04:03,308
None.

105
00:04:03,908 --> 00:04:05,715
Fleming left in a hurry.

106
00:04:05,915 --> 00:04:07,429
And I was nosy.

107
00:04:07,629 --> 00:04:10,014
I lifted the lids to take a peek.

108
00:04:10,214 --> 00:04:16,304
Just then, a dusty breeze blew in from the lab downstairs, where another scientist studied moulds.

109
00:04:16,504 --> 00:04:17,537
I sneezed.

110
00:04:17,737 --> 00:04:19,370
The lids went back on.

111
00:04:19,570 --> 00:04:20,488
Mostly.

112
00:04:20,688 --> 00:04:21,653
Probably.

113
00:04:22,253 --> 00:04:23,994
The watch jumped forward.

114
00:04:24,194 --> 00:04:25,769
September the third.

115
00:04:25,969 --> 00:04:28,782
Fleming was back, sorting his dirty dishes.

116
00:04:28,982 --> 00:04:30,165
Then he stopped.

117
00:04:30,365 --> 00:04:33,420
One dish had a fuzzy, blue green mould on it.

118
00:04:33,620 --> 00:04:36,232
And all around the mould, the bacteria were gone.

119
00:04:36,832 --> 00:04:39,093
Legend says he muttered: That's funny.

120
00:04:39,293 --> 00:04:41,209
That mould was Penicillium.

121
00:04:41,409 --> 00:04:45,200
And from it came penicillin, the world's first true antibiotic.

122
00:04:45,400 --> 00:04:54,023
In nineteen forty five, Fleming shared the Nobel Prize with Howard Florey and Ernst Chain, who turned that lucky mould into a real medicine.

123
00:04:54,223 --> 00:04:57,094
It has saved millions upon millions of lives.

124
00:04:57,994 --> 00:04:59,701
And then, the watch stopped.

125
00:05:00,101 --> 00:05:01,980
I was back in the museum.

126
00:05:02,180 --> 00:05:03,873
Same rainy Tuesday.

127
00:05:04,073 --> 00:05:05,889
Same air conditioning.

128
00:05:06,089 --> 00:05:09,862
The watch was back in its glass case, like nothing ever happened.

129
00:05:10,062 --> 00:05:11,578
Then I touched my head.

130
00:05:11,779 --> 00:05:13,274
My cap was gone.

131
00:05:14,074 --> 00:05:16,914
And on the wall, I noticed three old paintings.

132
00:05:17,114 --> 00:05:20,236
A leaning tower, with a tiny red dot on top.

133
00:05:20,436 --> 00:05:24,598
A young man under an apple tree, wearing a very familiar red cap.

134
00:05:24,798 --> 00:05:28,879
And a messy London laboratory, with a red cap hanging by the door.

135
00:05:29,479 --> 00:05:36,179
So if you ever spot a red cap somewhere it really shouldn't be, in an old painting, or a dusty photograph, that's me.

136
00:05:36,379 --> 00:05:37,941
Changing history.

137
00:05:38,141 --> 00:05:39,280
By accident.

138
00:05:40,080 --> 00:05:42,493
Next time, I'm not touching anything.

139
00:05:42,693 --> 00:05:43,657
Probably.
